+# Design: Move off is-odd
+
+Ecosystem: JavaScript (CommonJS, Node >= 18). Version in use: 3.0.1 (from `package-lock.json`; it is also the latest release). Its only dependency is `is-number@6.0.0`.
+
+## Approach
+
+Rebuild `is-odd` as a project-owned module, `src/vendor/is-odd.js`, with the same single default export the project uses, and import it in `src/index.js` in place of the package (`require('./vendor/is-odd')`). Successor kind: `raise`.
+
+## The séance (differential test)
+
+Both implementations are loaded in one test file (fast-check with Vitest). For the one API in use (`isOdd`, one argument) the test:
+
+1. builds an arbitrary that generates inputs of the types the API accepts, biased toward edge cases (empty, zero, negative, very long, unicode, `null` and `undefined`);
+2. calls `is-odd` and the project-owned `isOdd` with the same input;
+3. fails if the results differ, or if exactly one of them throws, and shows the shrunk input and both outputs.
+
+While `is-odd` is still installed, it is the oracle. Before it is removed, record its outputs for a fixed seed as a golden file, so the test keeps guarding the new code. The live comparison skips when `is-odd` is absent.
+
+## Risks
+
+- A behaviour that the inventory missed. The test only covers the API it lists.
+- Differences the project relies on by accident (error messages, key order). Decide per case whether to match or to change the caller.
+
+## Order of work
+
+Inventory, then the séance against the old code, then the switch, then the guard (golden file), then the removal.
+
+## The séance
+
+A curated recipe (`is-odd`) wrote it; it was run against the real packages before it was shipped.
+
+Files added to the project:
+
+- `seance/is-odd/seance.test.mjs` (test)
+- `src/vendor/is-odd.mjs` (replacement; converted to `src/vendor/is-odd.js` for the project's CommonJS code)
+- `seance/is-odd/README.md` (readme)
+
+What it compares and ignores:
+
+- The boolean returned, or the error class when the call throws (never the message).
+- Inputs: integers, safe-integer edges, doubles, NaN and infinities, numeric strings, arrays, null, undefined, booleans, objects with valueOf, bigint, symbols, functions.
+
+## Known behavioural differences
+
+Found by running the séance against the real packages (engine answer):
+
+| Input | Old package | Naive replacement | In the generated replacement |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| isOdd("abc") | throws TypeError (expected a number) | false (NaN % 2 is NaN, which is not 1) | Reproduced: the replacement converts with Math.abs and checks the result, like is-odd. |
+| isOdd(3.5) | throws Error ("expected an integer") | false | Reproduced. |
+| isOdd(2 ** 53) | throws Error ("value exceeds maximum safe integer") | false | Reproduced. |
+
+Decision: adopt the generated replacement, which keeps all of the old answers. The project's own call passes positive integers only, so none of these differences is observable today, but a replacement that keeps them cannot change behaviour for a future caller either. No difference is accepted; no successor behaviour is adopted.
